/*
   Dual Timezone RGB LED Clock
   by Raina

   Board: Arduino MKR WiFi 1010 with a DS3231 real-time clock.

   What it does
   - Two time displays (top and bottom row), each in its own timezone, plus a date and a day-of-week display.
   - Time comes from an NTP server over Wi-Fi (NTP code originally from Michael Margolis and Tom Igoe) and is
     kept by the DS3231 between syncs, so the clock keeps running if Wi-Fi drops.
   - The DS3231's alarms (INT pin on pin 0) say when to change the displays: every minute at :00 for the
     time, date and effect, and at :55 for the temperature.
   - Every day at 12:00 (top-row time) the clock checks its Wi-Fi connection, rejoins if the router has
     restarted (retrying hourly until it's back), then re-syncs the time.
   - Each timezone is a UTC offset plus a daylight saving rule, chosen on the setup page. No reflash needed if
     daylight saving is abolished or made permanent.
   - 31 colour effects (30 from the LedClock sketch, plus the original clock's rainbow).
     The setup page picks which effects are used and how often
     the clock moves to the next one: every minute, hour, 12 hours or 24 hours.
   - Optional temperature on the date display at :55 each minute, from the DS3231 or a DS18B20 probe (chosen
     on the setup page, with an offset to correct a sensor that reads a little high or low).
   - Auto-dimming from a light sensor on A0.
   - Home Assistant: with an MQTT broker set on the setup page, the clock appears in Home Assistant by itself
     (MQTT discovery): pick the effect, show a message on the date/day displays, brightness, temperature...
     Changes from Home Assistant are temporary (memory only) until its "Save settings" button is pressed.
   - Everything printed to the Serial Monitor can also be sent to another computer on the network (setup page
     "Network log"): UDP text messages, one per line.

   First start: the clock opens a Wi-Fi network called "RainbowClockSetup". Join it and browse to
   http://192.168.4.1 to enter your Wi-Fi details and settings. After a reflash the settings start blank again.

   Start-up status colours on the top time display:
     top row red          Wi-Fi module not found
     top row pink         could not start the setup network
     top row yellow       setup network running (the day/date displays also show SET UP)
     middle row green     connecting with the saved Wi-Fi details
     all three rows red   could not join the saved Wi-Fi; the setup network is started instead
     bottom row + colon   joined the Wi-Fi
     all rows pink        no reply from the time server
     all rows grey        could not look up the time server

   Displays are oversized 3D prints taken from
   https://nerdiy.de/en/howto-elektronik-sieben-segment-ziffer-pxldigit-aus-ws2812-leds-bauen/

   Reset-settings button (pin 14 to GND): hold for 3 seconds to clear the saved Wi-Fi network and password
   and restart into the setup network. Other settings are kept.

   Network name: the clock calls itself CLOCK_HOSTNAME ("RainbowWallClock") on the Wi-Fi, so it's easy to spot
   in the router's device list, and the setup page can be opened at http://rainbowwallclock.local

   Serial Monitor (115200 baud): type help for test commands (pick an effect, show test text, and so on).

   GPL License applies etc etc etc
*/

